translation:
I say: The second station of this path is that the rightly guided one mixes with the common people with forbearance, pardons the ignorant, and shows compassion to all believers. Otherwise, it is to be feared that he will be tried — and refuge is sought with Allah. He said: "For that leads him to see himself and to act arrogantly toward others."

In "Ṣaḥīḥ Muslim," the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: {«"Man qāla: halaka al-nās, fa-hum ahlakuhum"»} — "Whoever says: 'The people are ruined,' then he is the most ruinous of them." It is narrated with both ḍammah and fatḥah on the first letter. And in it also, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ related: {«"That a man said: 'By Allah, Allah will not forgive so-and-so.' And Allah the Exalted said: 'Who is this who takes an oath upon Me that I will not forgive so-and-so? Indeed, I have forgiven so-and-so, and I have nullified your deed.'"»}

Ibn al-ʿArabī said: Every believer has a proof, and Allah accepts from him the good of his deeds to the weight of a speck of dust. And he said in his "Sirāj": Every scholar has a lapse; every knower (ʿārif) has a veil; every good has a limit; every sharp sword has a notch; every worshipper has a fervor; every seeker has a slackening; every traveler has a halt. He said this after saying: Forgetfulness is inevitable for the God-fearing.

Abū ʿUmar narrated in his "Tamhīd" with his chain: Before the Merciful — majestic is His glory — there is a tablet containing three hundred and fifteen laws. The Merciful, majestic is His glory, says: {«"By My Might, no servant of My servants comes with one of them, associating nothing with Me, except that he enters Paradise."»}

And in the Musnad of al-Bazzār and al-Ṭabarānī and others: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: {«"A righteous trait in a righteous man — Allah rectifies for him thereby all his deeds. And a man's purification for his prayer — Allah forgives his sins through his purification, and his prayer remains as a supererogatory act."»} And consider the narration concerning Jesus, peace be upon him.
